PART IV: LEGAL FRAMEWORK REFERENCES
========================================================================

This dual-license framework is grounded in the following legal
authorities of the People's Republic of China (PRC) and the United
States of America (USA):

A. PEOPLE'S REPUBLIC OF CHINA (中华人民共和国)

   1. 《中华人民共和国著作权法》(Copyright Law of the PRC)
      - Article 10: Copyright includes the rights of publication,
        authorship, alteration, integrity, reproduction, distribution,
        etc.
      - Article 24: Fair use provisions for reasonable use with
        attribution, non-commercial purpose, and limited scope.
      - Article 53: Infringement liabilities and remedies.
      (Latest amendment: November 11, 2020; effective June 1, 2021)

   2. 《中华人民共和国民法典》(Civil Code of the PRC)
      - Part III: Intellectual Property Rights (知识产权编)
      - Article 123: Civil subjects enjoy intellectual property rights
        over works, inventions, etc.
      - Article 505+: Contract formation and enforcement regarding
        intellectual property licensing.
      (Effective January 1, 2021)

   3. 《计算机软件保护条例》(Regulations on Computer Software
      Protection)
      - Article 8: Software copyright holders' rights including
        reproduction, distribution, modification.
      - Article 16: Limitations on software copyright.
      - Article 24: Infringement liabilities.
      (Latest revision: January 30, 2013)

   4. 《中华人民共和国专利法》(Patent Law of the PRC)
      - Article 11: Exclusive rights of patent holders.
      - Article 75: Exceptions to patent infringement (research
        exemption).
      (Latest amendment: October 17, 2020; effective June 1, 2021)

   5. 《中华人民共和国反不正当竞争法》(Anti-Unfair Competition Law)
      - Article 9: Protection of trade secrets and know-how.
      (Latest amendment: April 23, 2019)

B. UNITED STATES OF AMERICA

   1. Copyright Act of 1976 (17 U.S.C. §§ 101-1511)
      - 17 U.S.C. § 106: Exclusive rights in copyrighted works
        (reproduction, distribution, derivative works).
      - 17 U.S.C. § 107: Fair use doctrine (four-factor test).
      - 17 U.S.C. § 201: Initial ownership of copyright.
      (As amended through P.L. 118-39, 2024)

   2. Digital Millennium Copyright Act (DMCA, Pub.L. 105-304)
      - 17 U.S.C. § 1201: Anti-circumvention provisions.
      - 17 U.S.C. § 512: Online service provider safe harbor.
      (Enacted October 28, 1998)

   3. Patent Act (35 U.S.C.)
      - 35 U.S.C. § 271: Infringement of patent rights.
      - 35 U.S.C. § 287: Marking and notice requirements.
      (As amended through P.L. 118-39, 2024)

   4. Lanham Act (Trademark Act, 15 U.S.C. §§ 1051-1141n)
      - 15 U.S.C. § 1125: False designations of origin, false
        descriptions, and dilution.
      (As amended through P.L. 118-39, 2024)

   5. Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) Article 2
      - Applicable to software transactions as "goods."
      (Adopted in all 50 states with variations)

C. INTERNATIONAL TREATIES (Both PRC and USA are signatories)

   1. Berne Convention for the Protection of Literary and Artistic
      Works (1886, as revised)
      - Automatic protection without formal registration.
      - Minimum term: life of author + 50 years.

   2. Agreement on Trade-Related Aspects of Intellectual Property
      Rights (TRIPS, 1994)
      - Minimum standards for IP protection and enforcement.

   3. WIPO Copyright Treaty (WCT, 1996)
      - Digital age copyright protection.

